#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179529 :question/id #uuid "1cadcaa9-876e-56cb-b8b3-17695827c4f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7035" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7a6e67435aab40d5bf3630c66f79241008357e1549b1c77b4c0bdfea2b453883.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\dfrac{y\\,\\mathrm{d}x - x\\,\\mathrm{d}y}{x^2+y^2},\n$$ where $C$ is a part of the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $\\left(0,2\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a parametrization for the given path is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{′}(t)\\,i+{y}^{′}(t)\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{y\\cdot {x}^{′}-x\\cdot {y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}},dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4{sin}^{2}⁡t-4{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{-4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}{4({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,dt \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,dt \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179530 :question/id #uuid "5a63ca22-0b1a-5584-8b43-c80457016cd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7038" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=x^2$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ evaluate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x + (x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y = x^2.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,dx+(x-y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}((x+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(x-y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t+3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{t}^{3}-\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+1-\\frac{1}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179571 :question/id #uuid "4088c5c5-6f69-54f3-9029-3ba52f5b00d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9435" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2e4e808d8264685f9b88c5b0052870174d8be3ed2a7195149d45552118186a49.png)\n\nBy setting $y = t$ where $t$ is a parameter, find a definite integral with respect to $t$ that is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\, \\textrm ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x = y^2$ traversed between the points $(1,1)$ and $(9 , 3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y = t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx = x(t) = t^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle t^2 , \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179574 :question/id #uuid "d83c960d-aeb2-5c31-b26f-f17518220ac0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9444" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4d5f211c3bf3d306c6dc14205311c60eeaaf55de89f8af571ec0fa9e5add8a6.png)\n\n \n\nWhich of the above diagrams could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = xy?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -1,$ we get $xy = -1,$ which gives the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 0,$ we get $xy = 0,$ that is, the lines $y=0$ and $x=0$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = 1,$ we get $xy =1,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=\\dfrac 1x$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y) = -2,$ we get $xy =-2,$ that is, the hyperbola $y=-\\dfrac 2x$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/f9/f95c7ab30b7b02d67ceec039792e649a900dbece2896cc1e63341fcba9fc794f.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move from left to right in the upper half-plane $y > 0,$ and increases as we move from right-to-left in the lower half-plane $y < 0.$\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/3d/3d6be27e083bb2e8b508cca31671e5cb4ea4e39b366a99f1dd194a5ea8ffcd3b.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/99/996b5678396b4e9a91a4c55697442895331a7a6e4f8ec3eed550ea86845e44f4.png)\n\n       \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179576 :question/id #uuid "2eaebefb-6b18-5689-a746-0d5dc595991c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9453" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = e^{2x-3y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x-3y}) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x-3y}\\,i-3{e}^{2x-3y}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179580 :question/id #uuid "f025deb6-7c71-5d15-9966-52f7d5eeab80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9463" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0cebdd804ff244d037e14df6ec33efb86b522644f7b4addc688e27fd6a57e95.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} y^3e^{-x} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\ln{y}$ from $(0, 1)$ to $\\left(\\dfrac32\\ln 2 , 2\\sqrt{2}\\right),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x = x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = x(t) = \\ln{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle \\ln{t}, \\: t \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1 \\leq t \\leq 2\\sqrt{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints on the path.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y) = y^3e^{-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{3}{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u = t^2 + 1,$ $\\textrm{d}u = 2t \\, \\textrm{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{2}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{9}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{27-2\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179581 :question/id #uuid "d41ebc3c-f998-577e-a50b-a08faadd561f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9474" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9de4a1c2dfb6b8939b2440930eefb8b7020947bd7b943021214adf4cbfce735.png)\n\n    \n\nWhich of the above diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y) = 2y^2-x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $\\nabla f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =-2x\\,i+4y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(2,1),$ $(-2,1),$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $\\nabla f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(2,1) & =-2\\cdot 2\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=-4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,1) & =-2\\cdot (-2)\\,i+4\\cdot 1\\,j=4\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =-2\\cdot 1\\,i+4\\cdot (-1)\\,j=-2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors at their corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/b8/b88c62b154cecaace206248b62f72b8aa1f71662c0427adfbbabd5c2b4738a6b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179586 :question/id #uuid "b7d08596-fbe4-5601-a58e-dac90715f7e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9486" :question/problem "Find a definite integral that is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(1,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =i+t[(i+j)-i] \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1$ and $y=t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =0\\,i+j \\\\  & =j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x+\\sin{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179587 :question/id #uuid "17ec17a0-11d7-57c1-9e7f-6893ab9e51e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9487" :question/problem "Find the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+y-z,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}s  =  \\int\\limits_{t_a}^{t_b}  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1,-3)$ to $(-1,2,-2).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=-\\,\\mathbf{i}+2\\,\\mathbf{j}-2\\,\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t[(-\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k)-(j-3k)] \\\\  & =(j-3k)+t(-i+\\,j+k) \\\\  & =-t\\,i+(t+1)\\,j+(t-3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-t, y=t+1,$ and $z=t-3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-3)k \\\\  & =-i+j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = x^2+y-z$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(-t)^{2}+(t+1)-(t-3) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179590 :question/id #uuid "9d13f401-5e07-5b07-8916-df2b0b6b8f74" :question/math-academy-id "e-9515" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overline{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1, 1),$ calculate the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x + xy\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a} = 0\\,\\mathbf{i} + 0\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = 1\\,\\mathbf{i} + 1\\,\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\le t \\le 1.$ So, we have that $x(t) = t$ and $y(t) = t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\cdot t\\cdot 1+t\\cdot t\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179600 :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2.$ Write $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$ in the form $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179601 :question/id #uuid "d5a9cb43-6396-5153-a60c-3d653d61a47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9536" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d51d0cea4b75dffe5eef6ccf46e5156716eb1381d20b4193d811acfe83278094.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the curve and constant vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =                 0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                                 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $\\mathbf F$ works with the particle for $x >        0,$ while $\\mathbf F$  works against the particle for $x <           0.$ \n\n![](images/67/673798bb131da05a07b3f47894340cbbc143fa6a248823bcfbdf6b5a5238efb4.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$ for $x <                 0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/24/24884d31b2b9aa8d94b11bccfaab1fa31e62a34f4e5183676c79390923f1f243.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =      0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179604 :question/id #uuid "d844a573-49a1-59c3-bec4-e409c486469d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9542" :question/problem "![](images/29/298b7b4293ff104a62107670fa2aba603b8a23e9ad042f7e270a4acf6267a2fa.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <  0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =         0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >  0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                     0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/cd/cd71f379a02405bc21aecfb0bb14e9798cfbc4d7fd2c1d3a1d5221aa8d64638f.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2284e7ffa962fd46333c4eca47a77473007fdd96e0813b9928f0303df8170f3.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9471e9d-8fd8-54f6-b2a2-21979387ec44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177418} {:answer/id #uuid "cfaf38e8-dea0-53ca-af4c-7595b6aa19d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177419} {:answer/id #uuid "da81cbb7-1c0d-5fb6-a136-436cce0614d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t^{3}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177420} {:answer/id #uuid "1a417b80-1c7d-54df-a4b9-d83760ab35a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t^{2}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177421} {:answer/id #uuid "548acf66-02a4-5f9b-bf62-28fc428f02f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t^{2}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177422}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7770a3af-79d6-5207-9481-e0e095e0784b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177417}] :question/id #uuid "4163da76-7dc5-52f8-8a6d-5dcabcafe172" :question/math-academy-id "q-122139" :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)32xyds =-\\frac{1}{4}\\displaystyle \\int_{-8}^{4}AAAAAdt\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $y^{2} =-16x$ traversed from the point $(-4,-8)$ to the point $(-1, 4)$. Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814fc6cf-8415-51dc-aa71-2b2dcbf81962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254814} {:answer/id #uuid "86f05304-f4c1-54b6-a4c5-000eafd2bbb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254865} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b6b72a-eb09-5d1b-85c0-269d51d0e83c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255324} {:answer/id #uuid "be619077-ef60-58f6-93be-483e6d9cbf83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{51}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255369} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2dc83-c0d2-5f55-bd25-8b17407e5e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{61}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255492} :answer-field/id #uuid "50ce09ca-94dc-54bf-a873-caeb486ff0fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9a72d2a3-84b0-5b95-afa8-d5133b8bf8f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122271" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola ${y}^{2}=8x$ from $(0,0)$ to $(\\frac{9}{8},3).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=8x\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{t}{4}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{t}{4}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{16}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{24x}{y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{24x}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{24\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{8}\\right)}{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{24x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}3t\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}+16,$ $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+16}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{16}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{16}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({25}^{3/2}-{16}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(125-64) \\\\  & =\\frac{61}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b255ed32-00b2-5c02-8fdf-4137e45f1f94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x - y)i + (3y - x - 2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177454} {:answer/id #uuid "07aeabaa-1756-5d7c-b175-364dae9da70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2x - y)i + (6y - x - 4)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177455} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac9617b-4aa8-5c9d-9329-1e2364c1eed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x - y)i + (3x - y - 2)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177456} {:answer/id #uuid "56d77004-8bf3-5e3b-9345-4eb6281965f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2y - x)i + (6y - y - 4)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177457} {:answer/id #uuid "5732c3e7-79ac-51b8-b415-739696a1c978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(6y - x - 4)i + (2x - y)j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177458}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ca579634-59cd-5d79-9ef4-86d15c0bb68b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177453}] :question/id #uuid "9fe868ae-14fa-5f36-b879-4b4d02b56eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-122399" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - xy + 3y^{2} - 4y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba475e2-be08-57e4-adec-8abff6266246" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253761} {:answer/id #uuid "1b833134-f253-5096-a14a-d79dffc93bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253903} {:answer/id #uuid "1d588fd7-e3d7-55a7-9d28-9f3428789f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{2y-z}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253917} {:answer/id #uuid "69fb1b09-9263-5539-88e5-0d8613d52b75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{2y-z}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254624} {:answer/id #uuid "6e94b4d4-2cb7-572a-9f0d-7088c7794500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253761} :answer-field/id #uuid "421569dd-cfb3-59c7-8de4-b002df6d520d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "68db203b-e688-5d3a-9ec7-9ce2f2bf5402" :question/math-academy-id "q-122404" :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y) \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{z-2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(z-2y)\\,i-\\frac{2x}{z-2y}\\,j+\\frac{x}{z-2y}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179966 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4e4f1e4-9db8-5103-b0e7-6bfa97069629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/56/565dab4b729e587b63826dc9f39363eaf0e941b39188f745b71a336368ed0b4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177466} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d9eaab-74ea-5160-8a14-8de7f5af555c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e8/e863d2283ea97745102ab489bd7721ad8987c37f33b246023bb2549053f3ebf7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177467} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdb37f0-b0be-50ac-906c-f6836336a177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2a/2abd84e2cd0c55cdec95ccb21c8a75270b97d6b7a3bf8b80137e38ab198c24c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177468} {:answer/id #uuid "3667299f-b2ef-5465-9b35-0ad04230adb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177469} {:answer/id #uuid "71859476-7f1d-5d93-8176-0d32012095b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7d/7d9612ef402244aad3a71fb8f8de587eb4be546ad3c1718885c1bafbb1ae1066.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177470}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5efd63b9-13ac-5b6c-ab07-8031398f0949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177465} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2294730-1a63-56a9-975a-ed773eb5c601" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/7d/7d9612ef402244aad3a71fb8f8de587eb4be546ad3c1718885c1bafbb1ae1066.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387182} {:answer/id #uuid "354411d0-1865-5b34-bf67-4f56be47754e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387183} {:answer/id #uuid "311b14fe-ee07-5a20-a6a5-59a701d67acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/56/565dab4b729e587b63826dc9f39363eaf0e941b39188f745b71a336368ed0b4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387184} {:answer/id #uuid "0d3a365b-59f8-5560-9234-ece8c5e30b6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e8/e863d2283ea97745102ab489bd7721ad8987c37f33b246023bb2549053f3ebf7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387185} {:answer/id #uuid "b7e37acb-40d4-5fed-9de7-438853dac171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2a/2abd84e2cd0c55cdec95ccb21c8a75270b97d6b7a3bf8b80137e38ab198c24c6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387183} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac480a4-ae34-526d-b6ff-99c48416aa4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5a8a53e-8c38-522a-bf60-158bb8a0f977" :question/math-academy-id "q-122407" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =2x \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,1),$ $(0,2),$ and $(2,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,1) & =2\\cdot (-2)\\,i-j=-4\\,i-j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =2\\cdot (0)\\,i-j=-\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =2\\cdot 2\\,i-j=4\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/dc/dc36c0bfd1e089dc4e5017b4d8eb3fc342aff35ca6b8ce1943a209915592d83c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b73086dc-5509-5507-97a5-c7e3bfba2349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177472} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c2aec3-ab10-5812-a6f0-e32442afa1bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/2c/2c01770dd5222fc43a65dd92381917821c127d39a0055ca464582c829ef8d3fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177473} {:answer/id #uuid "cad68cfa-0e83-5847-b359-8bce8928018f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c4/c4fa5e55308b46106812c7a3c2620b528487b520ea282144cc6baa2fd3cb345b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177474} {:answer/id #uuid "f4af8d52-9d50-500b-9881-dac55517541e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/41/417f9bd99ad7032ffac7ce258c5122d932b1bb1610237ef030f077ca56815bec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177475} {:answer/id #uuid "3ec2d219-5a14-5c00-a4d6-e94fc02507d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177472} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fd1fc14-019a-5f80-944a-ad3518a49e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43a11e4f-11c8-502a-b54b-73b03e51f3ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-122413" :question/problem "![](images/11/11c759098d959c7dbdcb81686640bfc6a01d4dc0f9b12e6ff45f3028ddc4d2db.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179969 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cfcc5e2-5fae-5e85-98a0-4b4a8389615e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177484} {:answer/id #uuid "b60f042f-515e-55c8-9669-504d7122f51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t^{5}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177485} {:answer/id #uuid "fa827bf5-62aa-53a4-8c0b-0bb5bf842b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{16}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177486} {:answer/id #uuid "275b43e3-32aa-59d3-b6f6-d5224224ccce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}t^{5}\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177487} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6793c3-401c-517a-8cdb-2cd95d932197" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{16}t^{5}\\sqrt{36t^{4} + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177488}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6218cae7-f715-5e6e-b9dc-dfe12bb38c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177483}] :question/id #uuid "8b50d48f-8fc4-5b58-ae9a-175e59cd59d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-122446" :question/problem "![](images/dd/ddefdc9ada8270723d9be7ab7fe3f0b2a59b73fbe69d38b674f2fa5c43800e4c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)xy^{2}ds,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x = 2y^{3}$ traversed between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(16, 2)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y = t$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "175c18fc-c927-5f10-aba9-f8c07cd1f45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/22/229bb0ca171a2f77f16e15471bce4037e642299e7141025bfb178a81f901ee02.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253871} {:answer/id #uuid "4b15e5a9-3b6b-5a1d-98c3-3f8c03d6c410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254316} {:answer/id #uuid "81f53b31-d962-5549-8632-88d0cfde19be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d5/d55feb791b51d0b4ebfe6b8998f1aad1e1a8d57d1593097b0c99d706a01d47a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254822} {:answer/id #uuid "c16229c8-fca2-5a01-8eeb-0140284c0d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/37/372482df951dbb50d6db837939a0708a9a69ea330c03e59bce19398575ad50f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255395} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc85735-898d-52d1-b585-43d59e88f534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c3/c3f79f33b0a983f7c92acebaddbd3bc5208275cca585ac5464ddf44c9a7e8d87.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255636}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254316} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ac1c634-c613-5135-9d7d-f12696fc2a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "50f7bc31-3a53-5257-80a4-a0a85f0ea7fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-122572" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by plotting some level curves of the function:\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=0,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0,$ which gives the origin $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=1,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ a circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$\n\n- Setting $f(x,y)=4,$ we get ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ a circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nContinuing in this way, we get the following contour plot:\n\n![](images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png)\n\nNote that $f(x,y)$ increases as we move away from the origin.\n\nNow, we recall the following facts:\n\n- The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\n- Moreover, the vectors point in the direction where $f(x,y)$ increases.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add the vector field to the contour plot, giving the following picture.\n\n![](images/4b/4b20823340d89e0ff4aa2fd65078d3819e8b218419a54ea632c1109e698858ab.png)\n\nSo, the corresponding gradient field is as follows:\n\n![](images/cf/cf6f0f97f5b54b95519319242e4f3867ae2ac0edafd5e2ab6debdfba3e519401.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967cb4cb-b2bc-5b09-a044-cead97fab3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{144}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177502} {:answer/id #uuid "124e4ecc-80db-5945-b2a0-0a70bacf7766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177503} {:answer/id #uuid "35a85fe9-c0c0-5c48-acbe-861e57fb6043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177504} {:answer/id #uuid "637fe0b8-5f7e-55d6-8cf4-e9808987ffcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{213}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177505} {:answer/id #uuid "707fa4c1-45e1-573b-8f56-1b0a414f3bd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{149}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177506}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a0597155-ae64-5f5d-9a35-8dbfb090d8b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177501}] :question/id #uuid "a4f83414-82d1-57f1-a541-5f71790a0a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122579" :question/problem "![](images/00/004a94ad9a64973b8e8d3edae709c75f0b76ea1a013d86dbfa87476b85742fc7.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_(C)4xyds,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x = \\sqrt{y}$ from $(0, 0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2}, 2)$, as shown above."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179973 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba90d78-1161-52a4-93c0-d995aaaf7e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6c/6cf967e8a5d3bc7b2ac021414875f5221ef52790dfbdd6e214cccc7f0e8be2d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387299} {:answer/id #uuid "bb982230-23d8-541a-a311-793fcd335e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4b/4be9586aaa886ccfe092f93c106a5f7233509c798c9a90ecfda0363173dc7afb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387300} {:answer/id #uuid "6f167c07-25dc-5f9e-956f-2fe0933e7ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/95/9593ba00b964194c1d91549fe38a1334a478ffd6b724534c4512a11d19d683aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387301} {:answer/id #uuid "640a700e-3b7c-59e8-83e6-b88bdc953d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f8/f814f8c7e6d879cbe25e5108704e29464d0c39859371ee4fac3bfb3e72f90696.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387302} {:answer/id #uuid "09b9b72e-d802-5315-b38d-ee456018cf2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387303} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7fe3fc1-2c7d-5b68-96b7-12857eae01aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fe3ebbef-9b0f-50cb-ab18-6df5f7b1d83c" :question/math-academy-id "q-122789" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{y}^{2}}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-2,2),$ $(2,-3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the expression for $∇f.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-2,2) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-2)^{2}\\,i-2\\,j=3\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-3) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-(-3)\\,j=3\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\,i-\\,j=3\\,i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the vectors starting at the corresponding points and obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/26/26a5c0123d93231a800456e9f06f6ba7450861838c87d4d1b3ae77dab4cec6cf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c16fe8e-df28-5acf-80d4-30daf328aacf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253906} {:answer/id #uuid "5aca3d77-fbac-59a8-a8e8-f141fffb748d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254472} {:answer/id #uuid "6641d652-1c79-5594-8a40-67438b550538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254589} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a3d234-e957-5e8d-85be-bdebd0026797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255242} {:answer/id #uuid "edb11fd3-821e-5d36-891b-095d0de53681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}-t+2)\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254589} :answer-field/id #uuid "761e5ab1-caf4-5260-859c-054aab437559" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "09a46495-b2d7-513a-938f-504451202710" :question/math-academy-id "q-123127" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y,$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,1).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=2\\,j$ and $b=3\\,i+j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =2\\,j+t[(3\\,i+j)-2\\,j] \\\\  & =2\\,j+t(3\\,i-j) \\\\  & =3t\\,i+(2-t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=3t$ and $y=2-t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j \\\\  & =3\\,i-j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3t)^{2}-(2-t) \\\\  & =9{t}^{2}+t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{t}^{2}+t-2)\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fd85cf1-6e5c-526a-9bea-91297855754b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(2 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177562} {:answer/id #uuid "9a07dfd3-fada-503a-8d7d-7c5d05270d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 - 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177563} {:answer/id #uuid "9f33e2da-0a3f-5c94-ad41-0fb76655fa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 - t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177564} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1e4d45-b42c-5544-b771-2c4dec44fb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 + 2t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177565} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1d15f6-2c98-5679-a3a6-3f9a91e6f361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{11}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177566}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b2227ecd-9c94-5c91-8bd5-29870aa8a181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177561}] :question/id #uuid "471bb8a9-9025-5051-9cad-6b9d143c1f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-123128" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x, y, z) = x + y + z$, traversed along the line segment from $(1, 0, 1)$ to $(2, 3, 0)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "711177e4-868e-5ac6-a2b1-961ab8f94f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257775} {:answer/id #uuid "8860493e-bab1-52a0-9bf9-eb7d11745d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257977} {:answer/id #uuid "b78bc20f-293d-5acf-9926-a6d8bb185d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258386} {:answer/id #uuid "db0fb6b5-5dcc-57a4-a8f1-684f9c0bb985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258695} {:answer/id #uuid "f570bf13-b1d2-5f54-853f-bb075d272c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258695} :answer-field/id #uuid "d19b03b6-7a81-55e6-a56b-0d4882ea6475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fb44c65-3cac-5280-b910-d92f6181f8ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-123269" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),$ traversed along the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,3)$ to $(0,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+3\\,j$ and $b=3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)+t[3\\,j-(i+3\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+3\\,j)-t\\,i \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1-t$ and $y=3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3)j \\\\  & =-i \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-t)+3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02791ce3-0e59-59cb-a7c2-9c1f5f9b8efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256804} {:answer/id #uuid "190adee9-da90-5ae0-b72f-1d54cbec3732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256997} {:answer/id #uuid "560421a3-f826-5226-b6d1-b81db072eecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257522} {:answer/id #uuid "7390b29f-af98-55d8-908c-23b7a22da5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257794} {:answer/id #uuid "b236cd5d-f995-5fc3-910f-4a9dd21013f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258334} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c7b60de-da7e-5307-9f2b-34e67ae5cd00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec525dce-ff06-5bce-974f-75c3d14b6b37" :question/math-academy-id "q-124597" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx-{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({y}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{sin}^{3}⁡t-{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡t+{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd60488-a0dc-5fbe-ad61-99ef09f53fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257588} {:answer/id #uuid "7d18e2c3-2a49-58ba-9262-8615f99ff198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257886} {:answer/id #uuid "7df658bc-3949-564c-8f0a-ff4d65a8122c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257897} {:answer/id #uuid "9647de0b-9ead-58ae-98b3-078246de2961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258104} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d41240-8070-5238-af02-d0421b2f2cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258104} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8f3449-3ae9-55ef-ba7f-735d655fcb1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bccb8e18-5ece-5d84-8a75-003e818045de" :question/math-academy-id "q-124654" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{MN}$ traversed from the point $M(-1,0)$ to the point $N(2,4),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =-i+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-(-i)) \\\\  & =-i+t(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\,i+4t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=3t-1$ and $y(t)=4t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=3\\,i+4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4t(3)-(3t-1)(4)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4\\,dt \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4(1-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b67b2e53-a0c8-5a8b-ab4b-ba652386711f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177772} {:answer/id #uuid "ca7605c9-de7f-5827-a732-cd950df6aaf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177773} {:answer/id #uuid "461655a4-20ba-5050-8819-a2d4ac27734d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177774} {:answer/id #uuid "547ccee9-b44b-5235-8cd6-c0d5e5ab7f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177775} {:answer/id #uuid "78ac1024-eff4-5445-b74f-f7d6bacbd27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177776}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8b91b8c0-4c4a-59fa-a6e4-99f749af022a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177771}] :question/id #uuid "9b9b6def-9db3-53b4-b1f4-7747acfa7a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-125364" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b70d2d8824cfbebbff2807239cf9b98796b43e8cb0f74e7e43ae25eb28b79086.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $AB^(―)$ traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$\n2. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{C}F \\cdot dr < 0\n$$\n3. $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-C}F \\cdot dr > 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a436c1fe-9875-5164-ae87-aa8cc5576ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(y^{2} - x^{2})dxdy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177778} {:answer/id #uuid "b2cf854b-fd8f-54e9-82b6-a3ed3d261ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)y^{2}dx - x^{2}dy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177779} {:answer/id #uuid "075dc6a4-8af8-5cf3-ae6f-ca3c20cc6c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)x^{2}dx - y^{2}dy + 5dz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177780} {:answer/id #uuid "46551c98-a762-5158-93fb-2a0daa1f08f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)y^{2}dx + x^{2}dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177781} {:answer/id #uuid "5f3538cb-9aa2-5a37-a8c8-64931e534447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_(C)(y^{2} + x^{2} + 5)dxdydz" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177782}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e716d21b-fca4-5aab-a10e-a4a0863ac589" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177777} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cca469f9-9d74-51d0-bb17-736cc0709826" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387624} {:answer/id #uuid "24aad204-9d3f-5ce5-afc9-9b3f4ddb1e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387625} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6d6937-1596-5b53-be7c-105c2edfc2d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387626} {:answer/id #uuid "b514da25-81e0-57e6-abfb-611516b89449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387627} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab32d0d-88ef-53db-9401-63fa9e5d5081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387628} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc8d59d-86e2-52f8-86d1-3f8f25c5a255" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "287c4c83-1be4-5535-a78c-1deaf55cde76" :question/math-academy-id "q-125370" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+5\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
